{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed abstraction names a structural mapping (loss landscape → institutional constraint) but provides no causal mechanism connecting why a single agent's gradient descent on a fixed landscape is the same kind of process as a multi-agent institutional-constraint regime where frames co-construct the landscape. The target itself concedes that research-programme landscapes are co-constructed by the searching frames, which is a direct causal mismatch: gradient descent presumes an exogenous, frame-independent loss surface, while institutional constraints are endogenously shaped by the very agents subject to them. Without a bridging mechanism—e.g., a derivation showing how fixed-landscape assumptions can be recovered as a limit of frame co-construction—the abstraction reduces to a surface analogy, not a substantive abstraction relation.","counterexample":[{"content":"An institutional-constraint model where the constraint landscape is exogenously fixed by an external regulator (not co-constructed by agents) would make the abstraction structurally valid, but the target explicitly frames the constraint as ownership lineage shaped by the agents themselves."}],"target":"03ca24dc91e2b3c650c99d5d409949e05b090cc59e4b9614ab15b46fa610ae24"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"74726f3c56db69e62803f41c634cebed3c043d22f49a7f633ccec2eadf2d5d92"},{"attack":true,"case":"The dependence claim asserts that without the lineage-dependency tax there is no cost asymmetry between within-basin descent and cross-basin exploration, but this conflates an institutional allocation cost with the intrinsic topological cost of escaping a basin. The refutation clause is actually satisfied by the target's own framing: saddle-crossing costs are a function of the loss-landscape geometry (basin depth, barrier height), which is a property of the problem space, not of the ownership regime. A lineage tax may *amplify* the asymmetry, but the target provides no mechanism showing it is the *sole* or *necessary* source of directed bias—rival lineages could face different saddle heights due to epistemic landscape structure alone (e.g., one basin is deeper because it captures more evidence), yielding directed suppression without any lineage-dependency tax at all.","counterexample":[{"content":"Two research lineages exploring the same problem space where one basin is intrinsically deeper (more explanatory power) than the other: cross-basin exploration from the shallow to the deep basin faces a higher saddle regardless of any ownership or allocation tax, producing directed suppression purely from landscape geometry."}],"target":"a9ca79f1e6bb8708ec6adbd80b524a320927dc408d8526f40dc15844ad255f2b"}]}